course: dictionary
lesson: 14791
title: Slang and polite words
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Slang is informal talk that friends use with each other, and it is not always polite. Puke is slang. It means to be sick, to bring food out of your stomach through your mouth. «After the bumpy bus ride, Ben felt ill and puked by the gate.» Ben was sick, so puked means vomited.
explain: You can use puke as a verb (to puke) or as a noun (puke is the vomit itself). «Ugh, that smell makes me want to puke!» Here puke is a verb. It also tells you something is disgusting, so you feel sick just thinking about it.
explain: Choose your words for the people you are with. At school and at home with grown-ups, use the polite words: «be sick», «throw up» or «vomit». Puke is slang, so keep it for chatting with friends, and only if an adult says it is fine.
fact: Puke means to vomit, to be sick.
fact: Puke is slang, informal and a bit rude, so it is not used in school writing.
fact: Puke can also be a noun, meaning the vomit itself: «The mop cleaned up the puke.»
fact: «Makes me want to puke» means something is disgusting.
fact: Polite words for puke are «be sick», «throw up» (informal) and «vomit» (formal or medical).
exercise: «After the bumpy bus ride, Ben felt ill and puked by the gate.» What does puked mean? | Ran very fast | Fell asleep | *Was sick | Laughed loudly
exercise: «Ugh, that mouldy sandwich makes me want to puke!» What does this suggest? | Something is very funny | Something is very cold | Something is free | *Something is disgusting
exercise: Which word is a polite way to say puke? | Giggle | Stomp | Whisper | *Be sick
test: Which is the most formal word for puke? | *Vomit | Chuck | Yuck | Barf
test: Which phrase means the same as puke up? | Shout out | Fall over | Sit down | *Be sick
exam: Which sentence uses puke as a noun, meaning the vomit itself? | Please puke a little more quietly. | Tom will puke after the ride. | Ella wanted to puke. | *The mop cleaned up the puke.
